Computational thinking has emerged as a fundamental skill for the 21st century,
increasingly recognized as essential for all learners regardless of their future career paths.
In Austria, this recognition materialized with the introduction of ``Digitale Grundbildung'' (Digital Basic Education)~\cite{digitale_grundbildung}
as a mandatory subject for secondary education in 2022/23, reflecting a broader European trend
toward integrating computational concepts into primary and secondary curricula.

\section{Motivation and Problem Statement}
While numerous block-based programming environments exist, including Scratch, Tynker, Microsoft MakeCode,
and Snap!, these platforms present several challenges when deployed in classroom settings with younger learners:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Open-ended complexity}: Platforms like Scratch emphasize creative, open-ended projects that can
        overwhelm beginners and make assessment difficult.
  \item \textbf{Hardware dependencies}: Tools such as MakeCode are often tied to specific hardware (micro:bit, Arduino),
        limiting accessibility.
  \item \textbf{Lack of automated feedback}: Most existing solutions require manual teacher intervention for assessment,
        creating bottlenecks in larger classrooms.
  \item \textbf{Privacy concerns}: Cloud-based platforms raise data protection issues, particularly with younger
        users in European educational contexts.
\end{itemize}

Teachers, especially those without programming backgrounds, need tools that allow them to
create and manage exercises without technical expertise while providing students with immediate, meaningful feedback on their solutions.

\section{Goals and Contributions}
This thesis addresses these challenges through the design and implementation of BlockQuiz,
a web-based block programming learning platform. The primary contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Constrained exercise model}: A framework for creating focused programming
        puzzles with per-exercise block toolboxes, reducing cognitive load and enabling clear learning objectives.
  \item \textbf{Client-side auto-grading}: A secure sandbox execution environment
        with multiple grading strategies for I/O and graphical (Turtle) exercises, providing immediate feedback without server-side code execution.
  \item \textbf{Teacher-oriented authoring}: A content management system enabling
        non-programmers to create, edit, and organize exercises and courses.
  \item \textbf{Privacy-first architecture}: A self-hostable solution deployable
        via Docker in school networks, with optional guest mode requiring no personal data.
  \item \textbf{Internationalization}: Full support for German and English,
        with extensible localization infrastructure.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Target Audience}
The platform primarily targets children aged 8--12 years, a developmental stage
where learners can handle block-based logic, loops, and conditionals while benefiting from visual,
immediate feedback. Secondary users include teachers who author and manage exercises, and administrators who deploy and maintain the platform.

\section{Scope and Limitations}
The minimum viable product (MVP) encompasses:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Two exercise types: I/O puzzles and Turtle Graphics
  \item Ten curated exercises covering sequences, loops, conditionals, and simple functions
  \item A minimal CMS for exercise and course management
  \item Client-side sandboxed execution with configurable timeouts
  \item German and English localization
  \item Docker-based deployment
\end{itemize}

Explicitly out of scope are: full classroom management with detailed analytics dashboards,
complex simulators beyond 2D canvas/turtle, and integration with institutional single sign-on systems (SAML/SSO), t
hough the architecture supports future extensions.

\section{Document Structure}
The remainder of this thesis is organized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Chapter 2: Background and Related Work} reviews existing block-based programming platforms,
        educational research on computational thinking, and technical foundations including Google Blockly.
  \item \textbf{Chapter 3: Requirements Analysis} details functional and non-functional requirements
        derived from educational and technical constraints.
  \item \textbf{Chapter 4: System Design} presents the architecture, including the exercise model,
        grading framework, and CMS design.
  \item \textbf{Chapter 5: Implementation} describes the technical realization using SvelteKit,
        Blockly, and the sandbox execution environment.
  \item \textbf{Chapter 6: Evaluation} reports on usability testing with target users and
        discusses results.
  \item \textbf{Chapter 7: Conclusion} summarizes contributions and outlines future work.
\end{itemize}
